  "interpretation": "Zero-cutoff baseline overlaps every tail query (including crossing-chunk queries) with the initial 32-query chunk in all measured first and warm traces. Splitting the Phi range adds a barrier and a second batch; the two-thread zero control regresses consistently even without streaming. Meissel does not have this initial-chunk overlap and does not show a consistent split-Phi penalty. Hybrid reduces local tail work but cannot recover the lost zero overlap.",
  "limitations": [
    "Chunk clocks measure elapsed time, not CPU time.",
    "Split-Phi changes barrier, launch/join and chunk alignment together; it does not isolate a pure barrier instruction cost.",
    "Trace instrumentation can change scheduling; uninstrumented plain and original-binary controls independently confirm directions.",
    "Comparisons pair process medians inside each collection only; plain and original-control passes are independent cohorts.",
    "Does not isolate hardware-cache locality or atomic read costs."
  ],
